I've always taken it that the "keenness of the blade" is a combination of 
the natural sharpness of the edge and the magical enchantment. Thus, 
blunting the edge doesn't actually do anything to the magic, but it does 
reduce the overall effectiveness by reducing the natural sharpness; i.e., 
a +3 dagger can be dulled so that it *acts* as a +2, and the overall 
effectiveness is what is displayed when the object is ID'd.

Granted, this way of looking at it runs into problems when discussing 
maximum enchantments. Shouldn't a +3 dagger that's been dulled to +0 
afterward reach its maximum enchantment at +4 (because it's *really* +7)? 
I guess I just imagine that it's not the actual enchantment itself that 
vaporizes the weapon; it's the "keenness of the blade" overall, which, if 
it gets too high, ends up vaporizing the weapon.

